---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const cv = `${base}documents/Curriculum_CamilaFernández-9.pdf`;
const email = 'mailto:c.fernandez@geosmart.es';
---
<BaseLayout title="About — GeoSmart" description="Get to know Camila Fernández — geology undergraduate exploring subsurface and offshore geoscience through curiosity, technical thinking and scientific communication.">
<section class="page-hero about-hero"><div class="about-rings" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div><div class="container"><div class="section-kicker">The geologist behind GeoSmart</div><h1>Curious about<br />what lies beneath.</h1><p class="muted">A geology undergraduate building a path towards subsurface and offshore geoscience — and curious about almost everything else, too.</p><div class="about-motif" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div></div></section>
<section class="page about-page"><div class="container profile"><div class="profile-column"><div class="profile-photo"><span>Your field photo<br />goes here.</span></div><div class="profile-caption"><span>FIELD / PROFILE</span><span>Venezuela → Spain → Norway</span></div></div><div class="prose"><p class="section-kicker">Hi, I'm Camila.</p><h2>I like questions that need to be solved.</h2><p>I've always been a science girl. I want to understand how things work, why they happen and what we can learn from them. For me, geology brings all of that together: observation, evidence, chemistry, history, problem-solving and a healthy amount of curiosity.</p><p>My interest in geology was shaped early on by growing up around the petroleum world and, above all, by my father, who was my first inspiration to look at it with curiosity. I was fascinated by the idea that something happening kilometres beneath our feet — under very specific geological and chemical conditions — could eventually become part of the world we live in.</p><p>That connection between what happens deep underground and what happens in our everyday lives is still one of the things I find most fascinating about geoscience.</p>
<div class="current-box"><div class="section-kicker">Currently · Trondheim, Norway</div><h3>Building my subsurface toolkit.</h3><p>I'm studying at <strong>NTNU during 2026–27</strong>, complementing my Geology degree at the Universidad Complutense de Madrid with petroleum-focused geoscience coursework. I'm particularly interested in subsurface geology, geophysics, sedimentology, geochemistry, seismic interpretation, petrophysics and geological reservoirs.</p><div class="current-tags"><span>Subsurface</span><span>Geophysics</span><span>Sedimentology</span><span>Geochemistry</span><span>Petroleum</span><span>Norway</span></div></div>
<h2>Why subsurface?</h2><p>My interest in petroleum geoscience comes from both curiosity and perspective. The resources beneath our feet have played an enormous role in shaping modern society and technological development. Understanding how geological systems create, store and transform these resources is, to me, a fascinating intersection of geology, chemistry and physics.</p><p>I want to understand those systems properly — not simply what we extract from the Earth, but the geological story that makes it possible in the first place.</p><p>That is why I'm particularly interested in <strong>subsurface and offshore geoscience, with a long-term interest in the North Sea</strong>. Norway has fascinated me since I was young, and studying here now feels like a natural continuation of that curiosity.</p>
<h2>What I'm building</h2><div class="focus-grid"><article class="focus-card"><div class="card-tag">01 / Subsurface</div><h3>Petroleum geology</h3><p>Understanding the geological systems beneath the surface and the conditions that make them possible.</p></article><article class="focus-card"><div class="card-tag">02 / Geophysics</div><h3>Seismic interpretation</h3><p>Using indirect evidence to investigate structures and understand what cannot be seen directly.</p></article><article class="focus-card"><div class="card-tag">03 / Sediments</div><h3>Sedimentology &amp; geochemistry</h3><p>Reading geological records and understanding the physical and chemical processes behind them.</p></article><article class="focus-card"><div class="card-tag">04 / Mapping</div><h3>GIS &amp; field geology</h3><p>Combining observations, spatial data and geological reasoning to build a clearer picture of the Earth.</p></article></div>
<h2>More than geology</h2><p>I like challenging myself and proving to myself that I can do more than I thought I could. My experience has taken me beyond purely academic geology and into <strong>project coordination, technical documentation, client and cross-team communication, international business support</strong> and digital operations.</p><p>I genuinely enjoy working. I'm resilient, curious, organised and attentive to processes. I tend to stay calm when things become stressful, I like understanding the bigger picture and I'm always looking for ways to make my work more useful to the people around me.</p><p>Those experiences have taught me that technical knowledge becomes even more valuable when it can be communicated clearly, organised effectively and turned into something other people can use.</p>
<h2>From Venezuela to Spain to Norway</h2><p>Moving from Venezuela to Spain as a teenager and now studying in Norway has made adaptability a natural part of who I am. Living in different countries has taught me to navigate new environments, communicate across cultures and remain open to change.</p><p>More importantly, it has given me perspective. I feel incredibly lucky to have had these experiences. They are not just places I've lived — they are part of the person I've become.</p>
<h2>Geology should make people curious</h2><p>I created GeoSmart because geology is one of the most fundamental sciences for understanding the world around us, yet its reach is often invisible to people who do not study it.</p><p>Through science outreach and <strong>@_geomila_</strong>, I want to change that, even if only a little. I want people to look at a landscape and wonder how it got there. To notice the rocks beneath their feet. To explore, observe and ask better questions.</p><p>Most importantly, I want science communication to encourage <strong>curiosity and critical thinking</strong>, rather than simply giving people facts.</p><p><strong>Because there is so much more to geology than rocks.</strong></p>
<div class="current-box beyond-box"><div class="section-kicker">Beyond the CV</div><h3>Curious about almost everything.</h3><p>Outside geology, I love travelling, maps, mountains, languages, technology, music, books and theatre. I can spend far too long analysing a novel, a film, a character or a map — usually asking very specific questions nobody asked me to investigate.</p><div class="current-tags"><span>Travel</span><span>Maps</span><span>Languages</span><span>Literature</span><span>Music</span><span>Technology</span><span>Film</span><span>Theatre</span></div></div>
<h2>A little more about my path</h2><div class="timeline-mini"><p><strong>Universidad Complutense de Madrid</strong><br /><span>BSc in Geology · 2021–present</span></p><p><strong>NTNU, Trondheim</strong><br /><span>Petroleum-focused geoscience coursework · 2026–27</span></p></div>
<div class="contact-box about-cta"><div class="section-kicker">So, what's next?</div><h2>I want to see how far I can go.</h2><p>I'm building towards a career in subsurface and offshore geoscience while continuing to develop the skills that make me more than a geology student: scientific communication, project coordination, international collaboration and the ability to keep learning.</p><p>This page is the story. For the full professional picture — experience, education, certifications and skills — have a look at my CV.</p><div class="about-links"><a class="btn" href={cv} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Download my CV →</a><a class="text-link dark" href={email}>Let's talk →</a></div></div>
</div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
.about-hero{position:relative;overflow:hidden;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f6efe7 0%,#fffdf9 48%,#e8f0ec 100%)}.about-hero::after{content:'01 / ABOUT';position:absolute;right:5%;bottom:20%;color:rgba(15,61,62,.16);font-size:.62rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.2em}.about-rings{position:absolute;left:-130px;bottom:-160px;width:420px;height:420px;border:1px solid rgba(15,61,62,.12);border-radius:50%;box-shadow:0 0 0 25px rgba(245,130,32,.04),0 0 0 55px rgba(15,61,62,.025)}.about-motif{position:absolute;right:10%;top:28%;width:150px;height:150px;border:1px solid rgba(15,61,62,.14);border-radius:50%;transform:rotate(-18deg);background:rgba(255,253,249,.25)}.about-motif::before,.about-motif::after{content:'';position:absolute;inset:20px;border:1px solid rgba(245,130,32,.28);border-radius:50%}.about-motif::after{inset:55px}.about-motif span{position:absolute;display:block;width:5px;height:5px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--orange)}.about-motif span:nth-child(1){left:12px;top:45px}.about-motif span:nth-child(2){right:18px;top:20px}.about-motif span:nth-child(3){right:28px;bottom:26px}.about-motif span:nth-child(4){left:35px;bottom:14px}.profile-column{position:sticky;top:110px}.profile-photo{position:relative;box-shadow:12px 12px 0 rgba(245,130,32,.18);border:1px solid rgba(15,61,62,.1)}.profile-photo::before{content:'FIELD';position:absolute;right:12px;top:12px;padding:7px 9px;background:var(--orange);color:white;font-size:.52rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.14em;z-index:2}.profile-photo::after{content:'FIELD / GEOLOGY';position:absolute;left:18px;bottom:18px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.55);font-size:.58rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.15em}.profile-caption{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:15px;margin-top:12px;color:#7a807d;font-size:.58rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase}.about-page .prose h2{position:relative}.about-page .prose h2::before{content:'—';position:absolute;left:-34px;color:var(--orange);font-weight:400}.about-page .prose h2:nth-of-type(2)::before{color:var(--green)}.about-page .prose h2:nth-of-type(3)::before{color:#b69b55}.about-page .prose h2:nth-of-type(4)::before{color:var(--orange)}.current-box{position:relative;overflow:hidden;border-left:5px solid var(--orange);background:linear-gradient(135deg,#eef3ee,#fffdf9);box-shadow:0 12px 30px rgba(15,61,62,.07)}.current-box::after{content:'✦';position:absolute;right:22px;top:18px;color:rgba(245,130,32,.35);font-size:1.4rem}.current-tags span{border-color:rgba(15,61,62,.15);background:#fffdf9}.beyond-box{border-left-color:var(--green);background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f6efe7,#fffdf9)}.beyond-box::after{color:rgba(15,61,62,.22)}.focus-card{position:relative;overflow:hidden;transition:transform .25s ease,box-shadow .25s ease}.focus-card::after{content:'';position:absolute;right:-20px;bottom:-20px;width:80px;height:80px;border:1px solid rgba(245,130,32,.2);border-radius:50%}.focus-card:hover{transform:translateY(-4px);box-shadow:0 15px 32px rgba(15,61,62,.1)}.focus-card:nth-child(2){border-top:3px solid var(--orange)}.focus-card:nth-child(3){border-top:3px solid #b69b55}.focus-card:nth-child(4){border-top:3px solid var(--green)}.about-cta{position:relative;overflow:hidden;background:linear-gradient(120deg,var(--green-deep),var(--green))}.about-cta::after{content:'CURIOUS';position:absolute;right:-10px;bottom:-30px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.04);font:700 5rem/1 'Montserrat',sans-serif;letter-spacing:.08em}.about-cta::before{content:'✦';position:absolute;right:38px;top:25px;color:rgba(245,130,32,.75);font-size:1.5rem}.about-cta h2{color:#fff}.about-cta p{color:rgba(255,255,255,.78)}.about-links{position:relative;z-index:2}
